    43	Verify that Gradle can select the required Java 23 toolchain:
    44	
    45	```shell
    46	java -version
    47	./gradlew --version
    48	```
    49	
    50	## Clone and verify the project
    51	
    52	```shell
    53	git clone https://github.com/gunturmanjusha/SmartCodeReview.git
    54	cd SmartCodeReview
    55	./gradlew clean test
    56	./gradlew build
    57	./gradlew jacocoTestCoverageVerification jacocoTestReport
    58	```
    59	
    60	The Gradle Wrapper downloads the repository's supported Gradle version when necessary, so a
    61	separate Gradle installation is not required. The build selects Java 23 and enforces at least 85%
    62	line coverage and 80% branch coverage.
    63	
    64	Open the human-readable JaCoCo coverage report after the build:
    65	
    66	```shell
    67	# macOS
    68	open build/reports/jacoco/test/html/index.html
    69	
    70	# Linux
    71	xdg-open build/reports/jacoco/test/html/index.html
    72	
    73	# Windows PowerShell
    74	start build/reports/jacoco/test/html/index.html
    75	```
    76	
    77	## Run a Solution Architect code review
    78	
    79	Run commands from the repository root. The simplest command selects the review scope from the
    80	current branch:
    81	
    82	```shell
    83	./code-review
    84	```
    85	
    86	- On `main`, it reviews the complete repository.
    87	- On a feature branch, it reviews only files added, modified, renamed, or deleted by that branch,
    88	  while inspecting minimal directly affected unchanged code when context is necessary.
    89	- Feature branches are compared with local `main`, or with `origin/main` when local `main` is
    90	  unavailable.
    91	- Outside Git or at a detached `HEAD`, automatic review stops because no current branch is available.
    92	
    93	Automatic branch comparison requires committed feature changes and a clean working tree. This keeps
    94	the tested revision identical to the reviewed revision. Use staged mode when the work is not ready
    95	to commit.
    96	
    97	The launcher performs the following work automatically:
    98	
    99	1. Runs the clean Gradle test suite.
   100	2. Runs the complete Gradle build.
   101	3. Enforces the JaCoCo coverage gates and generates its HTML report.
   102	4. Invokes Codex as a read-only senior Solution Architect reviewer.
   103	5. Evaluates every applicable review control without changing application code.
   104	6. Prints the review in the terminal and generates Markdown and HTML reports.
   105	
   106	The evidence model covers seven solution-level categories:
   107	
   108	- Architecture and Design
   109	- Code Quality and Maintainability
   110	- API and Integration Design
   111	- Security and Data Protection
   112	- Reliability and Operational Readiness
   113	- Data and Persistence
   114	- Testing and Verification
   115	
   116	### Open the code-review report
   117	
   118	The main architect-facing result is:
   119	
   120	```text
   121	build/reports/code-review/latest.html
   122	```
   123	
   124	Open it with:
   125	
   126	```shell
   127	# macOS
   128	open build/reports/code-review/latest.html
   129	
   130	# Linux
   131	xdg-open build/reports/code-review/latest.html
   132	
   133	# Windows PowerShell
   134	start build/reports/code-review/latest.html
   135	```
   136	
   137	The report leads with three independent decisions—developer implementation readiness, architect
   138	review or decision readiness, and production readiness—and a color-coded overall engineering
   139	assessment. Dedicated sections separate developer corrections, architectural conformance
   140	violations, architect decisions required, and evidence gaps. Issue links jump directly to the
   141	detailed evidence and verification.
   142	Related implementation and testing symptoms are grouped into one root-cause issue instead of
   143	appearing as duplicate flaws. Each developer-owned issue includes a short Java, SQL, configuration,
   144	or pseudocode fix sketch tied to the exact class or method, plus the focused test to run. Full source
   145	files and unified diffs are deliberately omitted from the architect report. Architect-owned issues
   146	identify the repository evidence, required decision, recommended option, and tradeoff without
   147	inventing missing business or platform requirements. The
   148	report also shows the evidenced business flow and solution architecture, seven-category scorecard,
   149	compact verification summary with a clickable JaCoCo HTML report, and a clear separation between
   150	routine developer corrections and decisions requiring architect judgment. Every verified problem
   151	includes its control, classification, severity, exact Java file and line or method, repository
   152	evidence, failure scenario, technical impact, recommended correction, confidence, and verification
   153	command. Passing controls remain
   154	compact so architects can focus on material decisions.
   155	
   156	Every nonpassing result is classified as a **Developer implementation defect**, **Architectural
   157	conformance violation**, **Architect decision required**, or **Evidence gap**. “Architecture flaw”
   158	is reserved for implemented conformance violations or verified system-level architecture failures;
   159	an unresolved production contract is routed to architect decision and may block production
   160	readiness without blocking access to architect review. Independently actionable defects remain
   161	separate even when they appear in the same Java class.
   162	
   163	### Review scopes
   164	
   165	| Command | Scope | When to use it |
   166	| --- | --- | --- |
   167	| `./code-review` | Automatically selected from branch | Review the complete repository on `main`; on a feature branch, review only its changes against `main`. |
   168	| `./code-review --repository` | Complete current repository | Explicitly review the whole repository from any branch. |
   169	| `./code-review --staged` | Staged Git changes | Review changes added with `git add` before committing. |
   170	| `./code-review main` | Current branch compared with `main` | Explicitly select `main` as the comparison base. |
   171	| `./code-review origin/main` | Current branch compared with `origin/main` | Compare with the locally available remote-tracking baseline. |
   172	
   173	For branch comparison, the launcher identifies and displays the currently checked-out feature
   174	branch. When an explicit argument is used, `main` or `origin/main` is the **base revision**, not the
   175	feature branch name. The base revision must already exist locally; the launcher does not fetch or
   176	change Git state. Unchanged files may be inspected only for directly affected context; unrelated
   177	pre-existing code is outside the review scope. The full build and test suite still runs to detect
   178	regressions caused by the feature.
   179	
   180	### Feature-branch review workflow
   181	
   182	```shell
   183	git switch main
   184	git pull
   185	git switch -c feature/your-change
   186	
   187	# Implement and test the feature.
   188	./gradlew clean test
   189	
   190	# Commit the feature so branch-comparison mode can review it through HEAD.
   191	git add .
   192	git commit -m "Describe the feature"
   193	
   194	# The checked-out feature branch and main comparison base are detected automatically.
   195	./code-review
   196	
   197	# macOS: open the architect report.
   198	open build/reports/code-review/latest.html
   199	```
   200	
   201	To review work before committing, stage the intended files and use staged mode:
   202	
   203	```shell
   204	git add path/to/changed/files
   205	./code-review --staged
   206	open build/reports/code-review/latest.html
   207	```
   208	
   209	### Generated reports
   210	
   211	| Path | Purpose |
   212	| --- | --- |
   213	| `build/reports/code-review/latest.html` | Final, readable architect report from the most recent review. |
   214	| `build/reports/code-review/latest.md` | Markdown source for the same review. |
   215	| `build/reports/code-review/code-review-<timestamp>.html` | Timestamped HTML audit copy. |
   216	| `build/reports/code-review/code-review-<timestamp>.md` | Timestamped Markdown audit copy. |
   217	| `build/reports/code-review/verification-<timestamp>.log` | Complete build and test evidence used by the reviewer. |
   218	| `build/reports/jacoco/test/html/index.html` | Human-readable test coverage report. |
   219	
   220	Architects use `latest.html` and its JaCoCo link. Detailed Gradle output remains in the timestamped
   221	verification log for audit or troubleshooting and is intentionally omitted from the architect report.
   222	
   223	The `build/` directory is intentionally ignored by Git because reports are generated results tied to
   224	one checkout, branch, and execution time. Anyone who clones the repository can reproduce current
   225	reports by running `./code-review`. Reports are not automatically visible in the GitHub source tree.
   226	
   227	### Understanding the decision
   228	
   229	The HTML report explains whether the work is ready for normal architect approval, needs follow-up,
   230	requires changes, must be rejected, or lacks enough evidence. Serious verified findings and build,
   231	test, or coverage failures override a high numerical score. The standards percentage means the
   232	percentage of verified applicable controls satisfied; it is not the percentage of source code that
   233	is correct, secure, tested, or production-ready. Each category shows its score, baseline, evidence
   234	coverage, result, and a concise architect summary without repeating raw control counts.
   235	
   236	### Troubleshooting the review command
   237	
   238	- **`codex: command not found`:** install Codex, open a new terminal, and run `codex --version`.
   239	- **Codex is not authenticated:** run `codex login status`, followed by `codex login` if required.
   240	- **Java toolchain not found:** install JDK 23 and verify it with `java -version` and
   241	  `./gradlew --version`.
   242	- **`Permission denied: ./code-review`:** run `chmod +x code-review gradlew` on macOS/Linux.
   243	- **Staged mode reports no changes:** stage the intended files with `git add` first.
   244	- **Automatic review requires a named Git branch:** check out `main` for a complete repository
   245	  review or a feature branch for a change-only review.
   246	- **Branch comparison requires a clean working tree:** commit the feature changes, or stage the
   247	  intended changes and run `./code-review --staged`.
   248	- **No committed branch changes were found:** confirm the feature commit exists with
   249	  `git log --oneline main..HEAD`.
   250	- **Base revision does not exist:** fetch it yourself if appropriate, then confirm with
   251	  `git rev-parse --verify main` or use the correct local base revision.
   252	- **No report appears:** inspect the terminal error and the preserved timestamped transcript under
   253	  `build/reports/code-review/`. The launcher returns a nonzero status for execution failures and
   254	  preserves partial output when possible.
   255	- **Windows:** run `./code-review` from Git Bash or WSL. Use `gradlew.bat` when running Gradle
   256	  directly from Command Prompt or PowerShell.
   257	
   258	## Build and test commands
   259	
   260	```shell
   261	./gradlew clean test
   262	./gradlew build
   263	./gradlew jacocoTestCoverageVerification jacocoTestReport
   264	```
   265	
   266	## Run locally
   267	
   268	```shell
   269	./gradlew bootRun
   270	```
   271	
   272	The default `local` profile starts the API at `http://localhost:8080/api/orders`. Health information
   273	is public at `http://localhost:8080/actuator/health`; the local-only H2 console is available at
   274	`http://localhost:8080/h2-console` with JDBC URL
   275	`jdbc:h2:mem:orders`, user `sa`, and an empty password.
   276	
   277	Read endpoints are public. Write endpoints require HTTP Basic authentication. The local
   278	demonstration account is `reviewer` with password `review-demo-only`; override it before a shared
   279	demo by setting `ORDER_ADMIN_PASSWORD`. This fallback is not used by the production profile.
   280	
   281	Three demonstration orders are loaded into the in-memory H2 database at startup from
   282	`src/main/resources/data.sql`. Changes persist while the application is running and reset to
   283	the sample data after a restart. Flyway creates the schema from
   284	`src/main/resources/db/migration/V1__create_customer_orders.sql`; Hibernate only validates it.
   285	
   286	## Production profile
   287	
   288	The `prod` profile uses PostgreSQL, disables seed data and the H2 console, validates the schema,
   289	and acts as a stateless OAuth 2.0 JWT resource server. It reads deployment configuration from the
   290	environment:
   291	
   292	```shell
   293	export SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE=prod
   294	export DB_URL='jdbc:postgresql://localhost:5432/orders'
   295	export DB_USERNAME='orders_app'
   296	export DB_PASSWORD='replace-me'
   297	export OIDC_ISSUER_URI='https://identity.example.com/realms/orders'
   298	export OIDC_AUDIENCE='smart-code-review'
   299	./gradlew bootRun
   300	```
   301	
   302	Flyway applies versioned migrations before JPA starts. Store the environment values in the
   303	deployment platform's secret manager; do not commit them. The identity provider must issue a
   304	`roles` claim containing `ORDER_READER` for read access, `ORDER_ADMIN` for read/write access, or
   305	`OPERATIONS` for non-health actuator access. It must also include the value configured by
   306	`OIDC_AUDIENCE` in the token's `aud` claim. HTTP Basic authentication exists only in the local
   307	demonstration profile.
   308	
   309	Production liveness and readiness endpoints are available at `/actuator/health/liveness` and
   310	`/actuator/health/readiness`. Readiness includes database health because every order operation
   311	depends on PostgreSQL; liveness remains process-focused to avoid restart loops during database
   312	outages. Both endpoints are safe for platform probes. Application logs include the sanitized
   313	`X-Correlation-Id` value in their logging context.
   314	
   315	## API
   316	
   317	| Method | Path | Description |
   318	| --- | --- | --- |
   319	| `POST` | `/api/orders` | Create an order |
   320	| `GET` | `/api/orders?page=0&size=20` | List orders, ordered by ID; page size is limited to 100 |
   321	| `GET` | `/api/orders/{id}` | Retrieve an order |
   322	| `PUT` | `/api/orders/{id}` | Replace an order |
   323	| `DELETE` | `/api/orders/{id}` | Delete an order |
   324	
   325	Example request:
   326	
   327	```shell
   328	curl -i -u reviewer:${ORDER_ADMIN_PASSWORD:-review-demo-only} http://localhost:8080/api/orders \
   329	  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
   330	  -d '{
   331	    "customerName": "Grace Hopper",
   332	    "productName": "Mechanical Keyboard",
   333	    "quantity": 2,
   334	    "unitPrice": 75.50,
   335	    "status": "PENDING"
   336	  }'
   337	```
   338	
   339	Valid statuses are `PENDING`, `CONFIRMED`, `SHIPPED`, `DELIVERED`, and `CANCELLED`.
   340	
   341	`POST` creates a new order each time and is intentionally non-idempotent; clients must not retry
   342	it blindly. Create, retrieve, and update responses include an `ETag` containing the entity version.
   343	Clients must send that value in `If-Match` when updating or deleting; an intervening change produces
   344	`412 Precondition Failed`, while an overlapping JPA optimistic-lock conflict produces `409 Conflict`.
   345	Validation and known failures use one stable `ApiError` response,
   346	while unexpected exception details are logged server-side and are not returned to clients. Every
   347	response includes `X-Correlation-Id`; a safe caller-supplied value is propagated, otherwise the
   348	service creates one and places it in the logging context.
   349	
   350	## Package structure
   351	
   352	The root package is `com.manjusha.smartcodereview`. The application uses package-by-feature
   353	with explicit layers inside the `order` feature:
   354	
   355	```text
   356	com.manjusha.smartcodereview
   357	├── config
   358	├── order
   359	│   ├── controller
   360	│   ├── dto
   361	│   ├── entity
   362	│   ├── repository
   363	│   └── service
   364	└── exception
   365	```
   366	
   367	This keeps the Order feature cohesive while making controller, domain, persistence, and
   368	business-service boundaries explicit. Cross-cutting security configuration lives in `config`, and
   369	HTTP error handling lives in `exception`.
